<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adolf Lüderitz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1886">1886</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adolf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luderitz">Lüderitz</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> merchant whose purchase of the bay of Angra Pequena and its hinterland in 1883 led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to proclaim a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a> in 1884. His land deals with the Nama chief Joseph Fredericks were notoriously one-sided, measured in German rather than English miles. Lüderitz drowned near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> in 1886, and the town on the bay bears his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adolf-luderitz.jpg" alt="Adolf Lüderitz" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adolf-L%C3%BCderitz-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Franz Adolf Eduard Lüderitz was born on 16 July 1834 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, the son of the tobacco merchant Adolf Lüderitz and his wife Wilhelmine. After the merchants' school in Bremen he trained in his father's business. Between 1854 and 1859 he travelled among the tobacco exchanges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and took a post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, where his employer went bankrupt and the tobacco farm he then bought was destroyed in the Reform War. Back in Bremen, his marriage in 1866 to the wealthy Emilie Louise made him independent, and in 1878 he took over his father's firm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A trading post he set up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> in 1881 failed. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> merchant Heinrich Vogelsang he then planned a German colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a>, still unclaimed by any colonial power, as an alternative for the many German settlers emigrating to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. In May 1883 he bought the anchorage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luderitz">Angra Pequena</a> and the land five miles around it from Captain Josef Frederiks II of Bethanie for £100 in gold and 200 rifles, and on 25 August a strip between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> and the bay for £500 and 60 rifles.</p><p>The contract gave its width as 20 geographical miles, which both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luderitz">Lüderitz</a> and the witness, the Rhenish missionary Johannes Bam, knew the chief would take for English miles. The deal became known as the Mile Swindle, and Lüderitz was nicknamed Lügenfritz.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> at first hesitated to protect the purchases, but when Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> to take the coast he agreed, and on 7 August 1884 the German flag was raised at Lüderitzbucht. Lüderitz went on buying land until he owned the coastal strip from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, about 580,000 square kilometres, but his mining engineers found neither gold nor diamonds, and in April 1885 he sold his enterprise to the German Colonial Society for 500,000 marks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Seeking an income from his lands, he set off in July 1886 with three others to explore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a>, carrying two small boats to Nabasdrift. His boat was never found, and the last night the party was reported was 21 to 22 October. In 2013 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luderitz">Lüderitz</a> constituency ǃNamiǂNûs, its original name, and in April 2018 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> decided to rename the street called after him in Wedding.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adolf Lüderitz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1883 the Bremen merchant <b>Adolf Lüderitz</b> bought land at Angra Pequena, and on 24 April 1884 Germany declared a protectorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.08,8.81&amp;year=1867" aria-label="Bremen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Adolf Lüderitz"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5451 5561h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5451 5561h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.08,8.81&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
